title: Binance / TraderTraitor
page: breaches/binance-tradertraitor-signing-service.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: Per Binance's initial findings, a campaign that began months earlier reached an internal wallet signing service; about $6.3 billion left its hot wallets, and the FBI attributes the theft to North Korea's TraderTraitor.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: ~$6.34B | impacted, per Binance | On-chain: $6.36 billion (TRM), $6.43 billion (Elliptic)
stat: ~$2.85B | frozen or intercepted | Per Binance on November 3, subject to reconciliation and market price volatility
stat: 50 | signed EIP-7702 authorizations | Per Binance, TRM and Elliptic
The FBI, with the Secret Service and IRS-CI, attributes the theft to North Korea's TraderTraitor, as The Record and BleepingComputer report.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

Binance puts the digital assets impacted at about $6.34 billion. The on-chain analysts count differently: TRM tallies $6.36 billion over 10 chains, and Elliptic $6.43 billion over 11. The page says each loss figure measures something different, so it names a source for every figure. Do the same when you say them aloud.

On November 3 Binance said about $2.85 billion was frozen or intercepted, subject to reconciliation and market price volatility. On October 30 it had said about $3 billion was frozen on-chain. A later slide sets the other counts side by side.

Binance, TRM and Elliptic all report 50 signed EIP-7702 authorizations delegating a hot wallet to an attacker contract. EIP stands for Ethereum Improvement Proposal; stage 07 explains what the delegation did.

The FBI, with the Secret Service and IRS Criminal Investigation (IRS-CI), attributes the theft to North Korea's TraderTraitor, as reported by The Record and BleepingComputer. TRM makes the same attribution with high confidence.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: June 2026 | Engineer A downloads a tampered Homebrew installation
event: September 2026 | A fake job interview puts malware on Engineer B's work computer
event: October 20 | The attacker uses an internal AI tool to gain control over two engineers
event: October 29 | A container is deployed; signing service transactions from about 20:05 UTC
event: October 30 | Binance's first two updates; delegation cleared at 00:10 UTC
event: November 3 | Binance publishes its initial findings
Binance presents this as what its current investigation indicates, subject to further independent review. It does not separately say the June and September compromises were the same attacker. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every date on this timeline is one the page gives.

In June, Wallet DevOps Engineer A downloaded a tampered Homebrew installation. In September, a fake job interview put malware on a second engineer's work computer. On October 20 the attacker used an internal AI tool to gain control over two engineers, and found a container deployment token on one of their computers.

On October 29 the attacker used that engineer's stolen credentials to deploy a container and trigger a script, and reached an internal wallet signing service. Transactions outside the normal withdrawal process started from about 20:05 UTC. On October 30 Binance published two updates, and the delegation from stage 07 was cleared at 00:10 UTC. Binance published its initial findings on November 3, five days after about $6.3 billion in digital assets left its hot wallets.

Binance presents its account as what its current investigation indicates, with findings subject to further independent review. It calls the operation one multi-stage campaign, but does not separately say the June and September compromises were the same attacker.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

== Each loss figure measures something different
layout: table
row: Source | Total, as each measures it | Frozen, intercepted or seized
row: Binance | ~$6.34 billion in digital assets impacted | ~$2.85 billion frozen or intercepted, November 3
row: TRM | $6.36 billion on-chain, over 10 chains | About $2.7 billion by 00:30 UTC, October 30
row: Elliptic | $6.43 billion on-chain, over 11 chains | $2.85 billion verified frozen or seized
row: Chainalysis | Not given on the page | About $2.8 billion by November 3
Binance's November 3 figure is subject to reconciliation and market price volatility. On October 30 it said about $3 billion was frozen on-chain.
notes:
The page says each loss figure measures something different, so every figure on it names its source. This table keeps each figure with its source.

The middle column is the total. Binance gives about $6.34 billion in digital assets impacted. TRM's on-chain tally is $6.36 billion over 10 chains, and Elliptic's is $6.43 billion over 11. The page gives no total from Chainalysis.

The right column is what was stopped. On November 3 Binance said about $2.85 billion was frozen or intercepted, subject to reconciliation and market price volatility; on October 30 it had said about $3 billion was frozen on-chain. Elliptic counts $2.85 billion verified frozen or seized. TRM counted about $2.7 billion by 00:30 UTC on October 30, and Chainalysis about $2.8 billion by November 3.

These counts measure different things at different times, so do not add them up or average them. When you quote one, say whose it is.

== Binance's account changed between updates
layout: compare
col: October 30, first update | Initial investigation suggested a compromised DevOps engineer's laptop may be involved | Suggested it let an attacker reach a service container and submit unauthorized withdrawals
col: October 30, second update | Later investigation had found additional points of breach
col: November 3, initial findings | Traces the campaign to a June installer and a September fake interview | Presented as what its current investigation indicates | Subject to further independent review
The page names this change as one of three caveats that belong next to the headline.
notes:
One of the caveats the page sets next to the headline is that Binance's account changed between updates.

Binance's first update, on October 30, said its initial investigation suggested the incident may involve a compromised DevOps engineer's laptop that let an attacker reach a service container and submit unauthorized withdrawal transactions.

Its second update that day said later investigation had found additional points of breach.

The November 3 post traces the campaign back months earlier, to the tampered installer in June and the fake job interview in September. Binance presents it as what its current investigation indicates, with findings subject to further independent review.

Binance co-CEO Richard Teng also said, as quoted by The Record, that the attacker used a self-destruct mechanism that destroyed tooling and traces as it went.

When you tell this story, say which update a detail comes from.

== What Binance has not said
layout: table
row: Stage | What Binance has not said
row: 01 | The AI coding tool, or how the tampered installer was delivered
row: 02 | The job platform, or the fictitious company
row: 04 | What the AI tool does, its permissions, or how it gave control
row: 04 | How the token was stored, or how long it stayed valid
row: 05 | Where the container ran, which platform deployed it, or if it needed approval
row: 05 | How the container reached, and authenticated to, the signing service
CSO Online lists the token's lifetime, deployment approval and that authentication among the questions Binance did not answer.
notes:
Another caveat the page sets next to the headline is that Binance has not said where the container ran, what the internal AI tool does, or how the container reached the signing service. This table gathers those gaps, and the others the page notes, by stage.

Binance does not name the AI coding tool in stage 01 or say how the tampered installer was delivered. It does not name the job platform or the company in stage 02.

For stage 04, it does not say what the internal AI tool is for, what permissions it held, or how it gave the attacker control. CSO Online reports that Binance did not clarify the tool's purpose to CSO either. Binance also does not say how the token was stored or how long it stayed valid.

For stage 05, it does not say where the container ran, which platform deployed it, whether the deployment needed approval, or how code in the container authenticated to the wallet signing service. It also gives no timezone for its 17:15 to 19:56 window on October 29, although the other times in its post are UTC.

CSO Online lists the token's lifetime, deployment approval, and authentication between the container and the signing service among the questions Binance did not answer.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Precursor | Tampered install | T1204
step: 02 | Initial Access | Fake recruiter | T1566.003
step: 03 | Execution and Persistence | pyenv swapped | T1554
step: 04 | Credential Access | AI tool, token | T1552
step: 05 | Execution | Container deploy | T1610
step: 06 | Impact | Signer signs | T1657
step: 07 | Persistence | Delegation sweep | T1098
Arrows show the page's stage order. Binance does not separately say stages 01 and 02 were the same attacker.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase and ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, the precursor, is the tampered Homebrew installation in June. Stage 02 is initial access through a fake recruiter in September, and stage 03, under Execution and Persistence, is the interview assignment that replaced pyenv. Stage 04, Credential Access, is the internal AI tool and the deployment token. Stage 05, Execution, is the container and the script. Stage 06, Impact, is the attacker starting transactions through the signing service. Stage 07, Persistence, is the EIP-7702 delegation that kept sweeping a hot wallet.

Binance calls the whole operation one multi-stage campaign, but does not separately say that the June and September compromises were the same attacker. Read the arrows as the page's stage order. Stage 07's delegation transaction, at 20:20 UTC per Elliptic, came after the stage 06 transactions began at about 20:05 UTC, per Binance, TRM and Elliptic.

The page maps no technique to the AI tool's part in stage 04, because no mechanism is public; T1552 covers the token.

== In June, a tampered Homebrew installation
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Precursor
technique: T1204 | User Execution
fact: Who | Wallet DevOps Engineer A
fact: How | a tampered Homebrew installation, found through a search engine
fact: Response | device replaced, related credentials rotated
fact: Not said | the tool, or how the installer was delivered
Binance says the engineer's role, and his colleagues on the wallet infrastructure team, had still been exposed to the attacker.
notes:
Stage 01 is the precursor. Binance says an engineer on its Wallet DevOps team, whom it calls Engineer A, searched for a popular AI coding tool on a search engine and downloaded a tampered Homebrew installation to install it. The page maps this step to T1204, User Execution.

Binance's security team detected the anomaly, replaced the device and rotated all related credentials. Binance says the engineer's role, and his colleagues on the wallet infrastructure team, had still been exposed to the attacker.

Binance does not name the tool or say how the installer was delivered. As its reference for a tampered Homebrew installation, it links a SANS Internet Storm Center diary from May, describing a malicious search ad that led to a fake Homebrew page asking visitors to paste a script into Terminal, installing the MacSync Stealer. Keep the caveats: the diary does not mention Binance, and Binance does not say its engineer reached that page.

Binance calls the whole operation one multi-stage campaign, but does not separately say that the June and September compromises were the same attacker.

== A recruiter who did not exist
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1566.003 | Spearphishing via Service
fact: When | September 2026
fact: Who | Software Engineer B, profile citing his Binance experience
fact: Approach | a legitimate job-hunting platform, a fake Project Director
fact: Interviews | live video with a fake HR representative, then a fake CEO
BleepingComputer compares the lure to earlier North Korean campaigns, Contagious Interview and Operation Dream Job, and notes Binance has not linked it to either.
notes:
In September, Binance says, Software Engineer B was contacted through a legitimate job-hunting platform by an individual posing as a Project Director. His profile there referenced his experience at Binance. The page maps this to T1566.003, Spearphishing via Service.

The purported Project Director arranged a first-round live video interview with an HR representative, who shared a social media account with personal photos and videos to build trust. A second round followed with someone claiming to be the CEO of a fictitious company. Binance does not name the platform or the company.

BleepingComputer compares the lure to earlier North Korean fake-recruitment campaigns known as Contagious Interview and Operation Dream Job, and notes that Binance has not linked the compromise to either. Keep that comparison attributed to BleepingComputer when you repeat it.

The next slide explains spearphishing via service in general terms.

== What ATT&CK calls spearphishing via service
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1566.003 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/003/
Spearphishing via service reaches a target through third-party services such as social media or personal webmail, not enterprise email. ATT&CK says these are more likely to have less strict security policies, and describes fake accounts messaging employees about jobs.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a technique in general, from MITRE ATT&CK, and says nothing about the incident.

ATT&CK describes spearphishing via service as a variant of spearphishing that uses third-party services, such as social media, personal webmail and other services an enterprise does not control, instead of enterprise email. Those services are more likely than an enterprise to have a less strict security policy.

As with most spearphishing, the goal is to build rapport with the target or to get the target's interest. ATT&CK gives the example of fake social media accounts messaging employees about potential job opportunities, which gives a plausible reason to ask about the services, policies and software running in an environment. The adversary can then send malicious links or attachments through those services.

If a payload does not work as expected, ATT&CK notes, the adversary can keep talking to the target and troubleshoot with them how to get it working.

== An interview assignment replaced pyenv
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Execution and Persistence
technique: T1554 | Compromise Host Software Binary
fact: What ran | an "assignment" containing a malicious code repository
fact: Tool | pyenv, the Python version manager, installed through Homebrew
fact: Swap | the valid pyenv deleted, a new file renamed into its place
fact: Detection | none by endpoint tools at the time, per Binance
A developer runs a tool like pyenv routinely, so a replaced copy runs again each time it is used.
notes:
Binance says Engineer B was instructed to download an assignment containing a malicious code repository onto his work computer. The code replaced a legitimate development tool component with a malicious version, used obfuscation to evade detection, and established communication with an attacker-controlled external server.

Binance calls it 0-day malware and says the endpoint security tools did not detect it at the time. The 0-day label is Binance's, so attribute it when you use it.

The file operations Binance published show which tool: pyenv, the Python version manager, installed through Homebrew. The next slide walks through them.

The page maps this step to T1554, Compromise Host Software Binary. A developer runs a tool like pyenv routinely, so a replaced copy runs again each time it is used. That is why the page chose this technique.

== The pyenv swap, in Binance's published file operations
layout: table
row: Operation | What Binance's file operations show
row: Created | /opt/homebrew/Cellar/pyenv/2.8.0/libexec/pyenv_, beside the real pyenv
row: Deleted | the valid pyenv, 3,344 bytes
row: Renamed | pyenv_, 3,414 bytes, into the valid pyenv's place
row: Relinked | /opt/homebrew/bin/pyenv deleted, and a new symlink created
row: Spawned | a zsh shell, after the new symlink
Binance calls the malware 0-day and says endpoint tools did not detect it at the time.
notes:
These are the file operations Binance published, in the order the page gives them. They show which tool was replaced: pyenv, the Python version manager, installed through Homebrew.

A new file, pyenv_, was created beside the real pyenv, at /opt/homebrew/Cellar/pyenv/2.8.0/libexec/pyenv_. The valid pyenv, 3,344 bytes, was deleted, and pyenv_, 3,414 bytes, was renamed into its place. Then the link at /opt/homebrew/bin/pyenv was deleted and a new symlink created, before a zsh shell was spawned.

A symlink, short for symbolic link, is a file that points to another file. The page does not say where the new symlink points.

The table follows the page's order. It adds no times, because the page gives none for these operations.

== An internal AI tool, then a token
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552 | Unsecured Credentials
fact: When | 20 October 2026, from Engineer B's compromised computer
fact: Through | an internal AI tool; purpose and permissions not disclosed
fact: Reached | Wallet DevOps Engineer A and Wallet Software Engineer C
fact: Found | a container deployment token on Engineer A's computer
No mechanism is public for how the AI tool gave the attacker control, so the page maps only the token to ATT&CK.
notes:
On October 20, Binance says, the attacker used an internal AI tool from Engineer B's compromised computer to gain control over Wallet DevOps Engineer A and Wallet Software Engineer C. Engineer A is the same label Binance gives the engineer from June.

Binance does not say what the tool is for, what permissions it held, or how it gave the attacker control. CSO Online reports that Binance did not clarify the tool's purpose to CSO either. With no public mechanism, the page does not map that movement to an ATT&CK technique.

The attacker then discovered that Engineer A had a container deployment permission token on his computer. That is the part the page maps, to T1552, Unsecured Credentials. Binance does not say how the token was stored or how long it stayed valid, and CSO Online lists the token's lifetime among the questions Binance did not answer.

The next slide explains what ATT&CK means by unsecured credentials, in general terms.

== What ATT&CK calls unsecured credentials
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1552 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/
ATT&CK's unsecured credentials are insecurely stored credentials an adversary finds by searching a compromised system: in plaintext files such as shell history, in operating system or application repositories, or in files such as private keys.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes adversaries searching compromised systems to find and obtain insecurely stored credentials. Credentials can be stored or misplaced in many places on a system: plaintext files such as shell history, operating system or application-specific repositories such as the Windows registry, and other specialized files or artifacts such as private keys.

Its sub-techniques name further places, among them credentials in files, the cloud instance metadata API, container APIs and chat messages. API stands for application programming interface.

This slide explains the category only. The page does not say how the token in stage 04 was stored.

== A container deployed with stolen credentials
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Execution
technique: T1610 | Deploy Container
fact: Window | 17:15 to 19:56, 29 October; timezone not stated
fact: From | Software Engineer C's computer
fact: Credentials | stolen from Wallet DevOps Engineer A
fact: Action | deployed a container and triggered a malicious script
Binance does not say where the container ran, whether deployment needed approval, or how code in it authenticated to the signing service.
notes:
Binance places this step between 17:15 and 19:56 on October 29. It gives no timezone for that window, although the other times in its post are UTC, so do not convert it.

The attacker logged onto Software Engineer C's computer, used Wallet DevOps Engineer A's stolen credentials to deploy a container, and triggered a malicious script. The page maps this to T1610, Deploy Container.

Binance does not say where the container ran, which platform deployed it, whether the deployment needed approval, or how code in the container authenticated to the wallet signing service. CSO Online describes the token as allowing new containers to be deployed on the company's infrastructure, and lists deployment approval and that authentication among the questions Binance did not answer.

Binance co-CEO Richard Teng said, as quoted by The Record, that the attacker used a self-destruct mechanism that destroyed tooling and traces as it went.

The next slide explains deploying a container in general terms.

== What ATT&CK calls deploying a container
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1610 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1610/
ATT&CK describes adversaries deploying a container to run processes, such as ones that download malware, or one configured without network rules or user limits to bypass defenses. Means include Docker's create and start APIs and the Kubernetes dashboard.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes adversaries deploying a container into an environment to facilitate execution or evade defenses. A new container can run processes tied to a particular image or deployment, such as ones that execute or download malware. Or it can be configured without network rules, user limitations and similar controls, to bypass defenses already in the environment. Containers may come from malicious images, or from benign images that download and run malicious payloads at runtime.

Containers can be deployed through Docker's create and start APIs, or through a web application such as the Kubernetes dashboard or Kubeflow. In Kubernetes, workloads such as ReplicaSets or DaemonSets can deploy containers across multiple nodes.

These are general examples. The page does not say where the container in stage 05 ran or which platform deployed it.

== The signing service signs
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Start | about 20:05 UTC (Binance, TRM, Elliptic); about 20:20 UTC (Chainalysis)
fact: XRP | 233.46 million from five hot wallets, 20:05 to 20:09 UTC
fact: SOL and OP | about 970,500 SOL and 44.2 million OP
fact: BTC | 18,270 from one hot wallet
TRM puts most of the theft in about 20 minutes, starting at 20:05 UTC.
notes:
Binance says the attacker exploited unauthorized access to an internal wallet signing service to initiate transactions outside the normal withdrawal process. The page maps this to T1657, Financial Theft.

Binance lists the outflows. Five hot wallets sent 233.46 million XRP between 20:05 and 20:09 UTC. Solana and Optimism hot wallets sent about 970,500 SOL and 44.2 million OP, and one hot wallet sent 18,270 BTC. Elliptic reports that a hot wallet known as Binance 16 sent 28,006 ETH and other tokens worth $174 million directly to the attacker, signed with its own key.

TRM puts most of the theft in about 20 minutes starting at 20:05 UTC. Chainalysis puts the start at about 4:20 p.m. ET, which is 20:20 UTC, fifteen minutes later than Binance, TRM and Elliptic.

For anyone new to the tickers: SOL is Solana's token, OP is Optimism's, BTC is bitcoin and ETH is ether.

Binance's October 30 updates said it had found no evidence that wallet private keys were leaked. The next slide sets that beside how the analysts read the transactions.

== How each source reads the signing
layout: table
row: Source | What it says
row: Binance | No evidence wallet private keys were leaked (October 30 updates)
row: Binance | Unauthorized access to an internal wallet signing service was exploited
row: Elliptic | Evidence suggests Binance's own systems signed for the attacker
row: TRM | Evidence points to a compromise of hot wallet signing infrastructure
row: Trail of Bits | A compromised server allowed to request signatures can misuse a key without extracting it
Trail of Bits' point is general, not a finding about Binance.
notes:
Binance's October 30 updates said it had found no evidence that wallet private keys were leaked, and Binance says the attacker exploited unauthorized access to an internal wallet signing service.

The on-chain analysts read the transactions the same way. Elliptic says the on-chain evidence suggests the attacker got Binance's own systems to sign transactions on its behalf. TRM assessed that the evidence points to a compromise of Binance's hot wallet signing infrastructure.

Trail of Bits draws the general point that a compromised server with permission to request signatures "can misuse a protected key without extracting it." That is a statement about signing systems in general, not a finding about Binance's.

The defenses later in the deck come back to this, starting with an inventory of every service that can request signatures.

== A delegation that kept sweeping
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Persistence
technique: T1098 | Account Manipulation
fact: Authorizations | 50, one per nonce, in one transaction (TRM, Elliptic)
fact: Signed with | the key of hot wallet Binance 14 (Elliptic)
fact: First sweep | 167,464 ETH and 63 tokens, including 1.6 billion USDT (Elliptic)
Because the hot wallet used Multi-Party Computation (MPC), Binance says, no single private key could immediately revoke the authorizations.
notes:
EIP-7702 lets an Ethereum account authorize a contract to act with that account's authority.

Binance says the attacker submitted 50 signing transactions to manipulate the account nonce and establish malicious delegation authorizations. TRM and Elliptic, reading the chain, describe a single transaction carrying 50 authorizations, each for a different nonce so that one would match the wallet's transaction count. Elliptic says all 50 were validly signed with the key of the hot wallet known as Binance 14.

About three and a half minutes after that transaction, per Elliptic, the sweeper contract emptied Binance 14 of 167,464 ETH and 63 tokens, including 1.6 billion USDT. The attacker also used Binance 14's owner rights over another Binance wallet to pull 823 million USDC from it. USDT and USDC are dollar stablecoins. Teng, as quoted by Elliptic, said the delegation "did not pass through our normal approval controls."

Because the hot wallet used Multi-Party Computation, Binance says, no single private key could immediately revoke the authorizations, and its team needed a specialized revocation procedure.

The page maps this to T1098, Account Manipulation, as the closest fit: ATT&CK has no technique for delegating a smart account.

== The delegation, in UTC, as each source times it
layout: timeline
event: 15:59 | Sweeper contract deployed (Elliptic)
event: About 20:05 | Transactions start (Binance, TRM, Elliptic)
event: 20:20 | One transaction, 50 authorizations (Elliptic)
event: Minutes later | Binance 14 emptied (Elliptic)
event: 23:56 | Last sweep (TRM, Elliptic) and last transfer (Binance)
event: 00:10, October 30 | Delegation cleared (Binance)
Chainalysis puts the start at about 20:20. TRM counts six later sweeps, Elliptic four. Binance's container window has no timezone, so it is left off. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
These are the times the page gives for the delegation, all in UTC, each with its source.

Elliptic says the sweeper contract had been deployed at 15:59 UTC and rehearsed on another wallet about four hours before it was used against Binance. Binance, TRM and Elliptic put the start of the transactions at about 20:05 UTC; Chainalysis puts it at about 20:20. Elliptic times the transaction carrying the 50 authorizations at 20:20 UTC, and the emptying of Binance 14 about three and a half minutes later.

The delegation stayed in place. TRM reports that it swept newly arrived funds at six later times; Elliptic counts four later sweeps. Both put the last at 23:56 UTC. Binance says the final unauthorized transfer, 2,961.9 ETH, finalized then, and the delegation was cleared at 00:10 UTC on October 30.

Binance's 17:15 to 19:56 window for the container is not on this timeline, because Binance gives no timezone for it.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

== Six defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Trace every path from a laptop to the signer | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Treat delegation as an administrative privilege | 07
row: Limits the operational key cannot change | -
row: Rehearse revoking a delegation | 07
row: Short-lived tokens, privileged engineers | 01 | 03
row: Scope internal AI tools like service accounts | 04
No defense on the page is tied to stage 02, the fake recruiter.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the seven stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words tie it to that stage.

Tracing every path to the signer cites a deployment credential on one engineer's computer (stage 04) that led to a container (stage 05) that could reach the signer (stage 06). Treating delegation as an administrative privilege cites the 50 validly signed authorizations, and rehearsing revocation cites the delegation that kept sweeping until 23:56 UTC; both are stage 07. The short-lived tokens defense cites both footholds, an installer found through a search engine (stage 01) and an interview assignment (stage 03). It also names deployment tokens, the kind stage 04 found, but does not tie the recommendation to that stage, so that cell stays empty. Scoping internal AI tools cites the tool that gave the attacker control over two engineers (stage 04).

The page states the limits defense generally, for any account after a delegation, and ties it to no stage.

No defense is tied to stage 02, the fake recruiter. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end.

== Trace every path to the signer, and guard delegation
layout: compare
col: Trace every path to the signer | Inventory what can request signatures, and what each can authorize | Isolate signing from business systems | Trace how a compromised laptop or server could reach it | Attempt an unapproved action from each compromised system
col: Delegation as an admin privilege | Flag changes to account authority | Reject EIP-7702 delegation where an account does not need it | Keep digest-signing interfaces from getting around that rule | TRM: treat many authorizations across nonces as a sign of compromise
Steps are Trail of Bits' unless marked TRM.
notes:
These two defenses are about the signer: what can reach it, and what it will sign.

First, trace every path from a developer's laptop to your signing service. Here a deployment credential on one engineer's computer led to a container that could reach the signer. Trail of Bits' first step is to inventory every service that can request signatures and what each can authorize, keep signing apart from routine business systems, and trace how a compromised laptop or server could reach it. Its test is to use each compromised system's access to attempt an unapproved action, and keep evidence that no usable signature was produced. TRM also recommends separating signing infrastructure and approval paths by chain and wallet tier.

Second, treat delegation as an administrative privilege at the signer. The 50 authorizations were validly signed, and Binance's co-CEO said, as Elliptic reports, that the delegation did not pass through normal approval controls. Trail of Bits says signing services should flag requests that change account authority, reject EIP-7702 delegation for accounts that do not need it, and make sure generic digest-signing interfaces cannot get around that rule. TRM adds that a request for many authorizations across a range of nonces should be treated as a sign of compromise.

== Limit what the operational key can do, and rehearse revocation
layout: compare
col: Limits the key cannot change | The account's own key can still move funds or replace the delegate | Independently enforced limits, monitored delays, a separate configuration authority | Test by treating the operational key as compromised
col: Rehearse revoking a delegation | TRM: keep a tested, pre-approved way to sign a revocation | TRM: alert at once on EIP-7702 delegation in a treasury wallet | Map every wallet, even empty ones, to what it controls
Recommendations are Trail of Bits' unless marked TRM.
notes:
Two more defenses are about delegation.

Put limits on funds that the operational key cannot change. After a delegation, the account's own key can still move funds or replace the delegate. Trail of Bits recommends independently enforced limits, monitored delays and a separate configuration authority. Test them by treating the operational key as compromised: attempt direct transfers, delegate replacement and policy changes, and confirm that none of them can get around those protections, including by shortening or removing a delay. The page gives this as general guidance and ties it to no stage.

Rehearse revoking a delegation before you need to. Binance's MPC setup meant no single key could revoke the authorizations at once, and the delegation kept sweeping until 23:56 UTC. TRM recommends keeping a tested, pre-approved way to sign a revocation, and alerting immediately on any EIP-7702 delegation in a treasury wallet. Trail of Bits recommends mapping every wallet, including empty ones, to the assets and contracts it controls, so an alert can name what is at risk.

== Short-lived tokens, privileged engineers, scoped AI tools
layout: compare
col: Tokens and engineers | Both footholds came from code an engineer chose to run | Treat engineers with production or signing access as high-value targets | Restrict software installs on their machines | Expire deployment tokens quickly
col: Internal AI tools | Limit AI tool permissions as strictly as a service account's | Log the tool's actions | Test what it will do for a request from a compromised session
The recommendations come from experts quoted by CSO Online.
notes:
The last two defenses come from the start of the chain and from the AI tool.

Make deployment tokens short-lived, and treat the engineers who hold them as privileged identities. Both footholds came from code an engineer chose to run: an installer found through a search engine and an interview assignment. Experts quoted by CSO Online recommend treating engineers with production or signing access as high-value targets, restricting software installs on their machines, and expiring deployment tokens quickly.

Scope internal AI tools like service accounts, and log what they do. Binance has not said how its internal AI tool gave the attacker control over two engineers, only that it was used that way. Experts in the same CSO Online article recommend limiting AI tool permissions as strictly as a service account's, logging the tool's actions, and testing what it will do for a request that arrives from a compromised session.

On its side, Binance says it has replaced affected devices, decommissioned the related credentials and tokens, and rebuilt the affected internal AI tool and access controls.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can you list every service that can request signatures, and what each can authorize?
check: Have you tested what a compromised laptop or server could get signed?
check: Does your signer flag requests that change account authority, such as EIP-7702 delegation?
check: Would your limits on funds hold if the operational key were compromised?
check: Have you rehearsed revoking a delegation, with a pre-approved way to sign it?
check: Do deployment tokens expire quickly, and are their holders treated as privileged?
check: Are internal AI tools scoped like service accounts, with their actions logged?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether you could list every service that can request signatures and what each can authorize, and whether you have tested what a compromised laptop or server could get signed. Ask whether your signer flags requests that change account authority, including EIP-7702 delegation, and whether limits on funds would hold with the operational key compromised.

Ask whether you have rehearsed revoking a delegation, with a tested, pre-approved way to sign it. Ask how quickly your deployment tokens expire, and whether the engineers who hold them are treated as privileged identities. Finally, ask whether your internal AI tools are scoped like service accounts, and whether their actions are logged.

== No evidence your keys leaked. Is your signer safe?
layout: question
Binance's October 30 updates said it found no evidence wallet private keys leaked. Yet through an internal wallet signing service, the attacker started transactions outside the normal withdrawal process. What in your environment can ask your signer for a signature?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

Binance's October 30 updates said it had found no evidence that wallet private keys were leaked. Even so, through unauthorized access to an internal wallet signing service, the attacker started transactions outside the normal withdrawal process, and Elliptic says all 50 delegation authorizations were validly signed with a hot wallet's key.

Ask each person to name one system in their own environment that can request a signature, or one person who could deploy something that can. Then show the answer slide.

== Trail of Bits: a key can be misused without extraction
layout: answer
Per Trail of Bits, a compromised server allowed to request signatures can misuse a protected key without extracting it.
- Inventory every service that can request signatures, and what each can authorize.
- Test each one by attempting an unapproved action.
notes:
The page's answer starts with what can reach the signer.

Trail of Bits draws the general point that a compromised server with permission to request signatures can misuse a protected key without extracting it. Its first step is to inventory every service that can request signatures and what each can authorize, keep signing apart from routine business systems, and trace how a compromised laptop or server could reach it. Its test is to use each compromised system's access to attempt an unapproved action, and keep evidence that no usable signature was produced.

Here, a deployment credential on one engineer's computer led to a container that could reach the signer. Bring the group's examples back to that path: for each system they named, ask what it could get signed.

== What would you add for stage 02?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to stage 02, where a fake Project Director contacted an engineer through a legitimate job-hunting platform. What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page ties its defenses to stages 01, 03, 04, 05, 06 and 07, and gives one more, limits the operational key cannot change, without naming a stage. Nothing is tied to stage 02, where Software Engineer B was contacted through a legitimate job-hunting platform by someone posing as a Project Director, then interviewed by a fake HR representative and a fake CEO.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 02
layout: answer
This deck adds none, so any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against reported details: a legitimate job-hunting platform, a profile that referenced Binance, and two interview rounds.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 02, and this deck adds none of its own.

The group may argue that treating engineers with production or signing access as high-value targets, from the tokens defense, reaches this stage. The page does not say what access Software Engineer B held, so that argument is the group's own.

Test each proposal against the facts the page reports for stage 02: a legitimate job-hunting platform, a profile there that referenced his experience at Binance, a fake HR representative who shared a social media account with personal photos and videos to build trust, and a second round with someone claiming to be the CEO of a fictitious company. Binance does not name the platform or the company.

== Primary sources, part one
layout: links
link: Binance, initial findings (November 3) | https://www.binance.com/en/blog/community/3229153443759566763
link: Binance, Security Incident Update (October 30) | https://www.binance.com/en/support/announcement/detail/c5a8729a97ac486d963784d96c5642d7
link: Binance, Security Incident Update #2 (October 30) | https://www.binance.com/en/support/announcement/detail/d3306d9dca5243548c813b605e1d1751
link: TRM Labs, Binance theft analysis | https://www.trmlabs.com/resources/blog/north-korea-steals-usd-6-36-billion-from-binance-in-the-largest-hack-in-history
link: Elliptic, Binance theft analysis | https://www.elliptic.co/insights/binance-largest-hack-in-history/
link: Chainalysis, tracing the North Korea link | https://chainalysis.com/blog/binance-exploit-latest-findings-and-response/
